  <conditions attached="1">
   <condition type="1">
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Consistent with the Article III of the SBAA [or the Supplemental Provisions to the Project Document], the responsibility for the safety and security of the Implementing Partner and its personnel and property, and of UNDP&#x2019;s property in the Implementing Partner&#x2019;s custody, rests with the Implementing Partner.  To this end, the Implementing Partner shall: a)	put in place an appropriate security plan and maintain the security plan, taking into account the security situation in the country where the project is being carried; b)	assume all risks and liabilities related to the Implementing Partner&#x2019;s security, and the full implementation of the security plan.</narrative>
   </condition>
   <condition type="1">
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Social and environmental sustainability will be enhanced through application of the UNDP Social and Environmental Standards (http://www.undp.org/ses) and related Accountability Mechanism (http://www.undp.org/secu-srm).</narrative>
   </condition>
  </conditions>
  <result aggregation-status="1" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Annual Output Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator ascending="0" measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output Indicator</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Indicator 1: Number of new partnership mechanisms with funding for sustainable management solutions of natural resources, ecosystem services, chemicals and waste at national and/or subnational level.
Indicator 2. Extent to which legal or policy or institutional frameworks are in place for conservation, sustainable use, and access and benefit sharing of natural resources, biodiversity and ecosystems 
Indicator 3. Amount of POPs, U-POPs and mercury uses, and release avoided at project implementation and predicted at replication. 
Indicator 4. Evidence that gender mainstreaming and equal opportunities have been ensured for job opportunities and access to knowledge and training</narrative>
    </description>
    <baseline year="2018">
     <comment>
      <narrative xml:lang="EN">-	Public &#x2013; private partnership in the management of hazardous and municipal waste, initiative are needed and the government is moving in this direction. Technical and financial support to achieve this objective is needed.
-	Policies and regulation on E-Waste are missing. Policies and regulation on HW, MSW and HCW need substantial improvement and enforcement.
-	There are very limited actions in place to reduce the release of U-POPs and mercury associated to the open burning or incineration of waste. Currently, U-POPs from HW incineration and from uncontrolled burning of waste are by far the two largest sources of U-POPs.
-	Because of the very strong gender division of labour in this sector along with cultural carriers, far more men than women get jobs in waste management in Jordan.</narrative>
     </comment>
    </baseline>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2021-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2021-12-31"></period-end>
     <target>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">1- Launching of the E-waste Management Instructions.
2- Design and print the e-waste manual.
3- Training on e-waste dismantling and recycling.
4- Establishing e-waste collection sites and equip them with e-waste collection bins and signs. In addition to the development of the required SOPs.
5- To calculate the reduction of U-POPs caused from the replacement of the sub-standard incinerators in Hospitals.
6- Finalizing the handover of the autoclaves to the Ministry of Health (MoH) and Royal Medical Services (RMS).
7- Conduct an upgrade of the JUST incineration facility.</narrative>
      </comment>
     </target>
     <actual>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">1- The instructions are endorsed and were launched in February 2021.
2- The manual was designed, printed, and distributed to relevant stakeholders. 
3- A specialized training was conducted in August 2021 targeting different stakeholders in the field of E-waste management and recycling.
4- E-waste collection sites are prepared and ready to operate.
5- The project achieved a reduction of 10 g Teq/Year (2021) of U-POPs from the replacement of the incinerators with an environmentally friendly technology named (autoclaves).
6- Finalization of the handover of the autoclaves to the Ministry of Health (MoH) and Royal Medical Services (RMS).
7- The project initiated the procurement process based on the environmental audit that was conducted to the facility.</narrative>
      </comment>
     </actual>
    </period>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2020-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2020-12-31"></period-end>
     <target>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">E-waste regulations finalized -Complete the e-waste financial mechanisim and identify collection sites -Finalize the instalation of eleven non combustion treatment units for healthcare waste management - Conduct an environmental Audit of the incineration unit located at the JordanUniversity for Science and Technology - POPs project website formulated</narrative>
      </comment>
     </target>
     <actual>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">E-waste instructions finalized, approved and ready to be issued in the official Gazette -E-waste financial mechanism developed and approved by the Ministry of Environment -E-waste collection sites have been identified by GAM, MoLA and ASEZA -11 non-combustion technologies (autoclaves) were installed in 11 different hospitals in different geographical locations -	Environmental audit study for the incineration facility in JUST were conducted and finalized -Two Awareness videos were finalised and published through social media and youtube channels; one to promote best environmental practises to dispose of gloves and masks and another one on the autoclaves installation and operation -Project webpage was established to disseminate project information and facts</narrative>
      </comment>
     </actual>
    </period>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2022-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2022-12-31"></period-end>
     <target>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">1- Procure e-waste collection services through using mobile application targeting households.
2- Conduct the E-Waste Awareness Campaign.
3- To finalize and submit the Regulatory Impact Assessment (RIA) to adopt and implement a financial mechanism to support e-waste management activities.
4- To develop and finalize &#x201c;Extended Producer Responsibility (EPR) instructions&#x201d; to be endorsed by the Ministry of Environment.
5- Finalizing the JUST incineration upgrade.
6- Updating the national Healthcare waste management instructions.
7- To design, procure and install a surveillance system for the monitoring of a selected landfill for random burning.
8- Provide XRF devices for the spot determination of Chlorinated/Brominated plastic material in e-waste (WEEE).
9- To establish an online tracking system for the licensed medical waste and hazardous waste transportation Vehicles.
10- To compile, design and print the ESM manual of the Health Care Waste. 
11- Conduct a training on the Environmentally Sound Management (ESM) of Health Care waste Targeting Public, RMS and Private hospitals in addition to clinics and medical centers.
